@begin(section)

@title(Roadmap)

This section outlines what we plan on doing to democratize dependency
management. It may or may not actually be implemented in the future, but
represents a guide for contributors and users alike as to the hopes and
vision for the future of the dsolv developers.

@begin(section)
@title(Future Releases)

@begin(list)
@item(Tutorial-like help screens designed to keep people from needing to
switch from docs to cli and back.)
@item(Shortened versions of all subcommands, including documentation
updates.)
@item(Documentation and/or code on the topic of supporting the use case of
different architectures of the same package using prioritized indexes of
packages named the same with different contents.)
@item(Publishing dsolv to @link[uri="https://github.com/ocicl/ocicl"](OCICL)
and, eventually, Quicklisp.)
@end(list)

@end(section)

@begin(section)
@title(Future Features)

Firefighters need tools that can apply in many situations; similarly, ops and
DevOps professionals, for whom we build this tool, need a dependency
management tool that can get them out of dependency hell no matter what their
situation.

@begin(list)
@item(New subcommands: @c(index-add) and @c(index-rm), to take away from and
add to a repository index in an installation/removal context.)
@item(New subcommand: @c(resolve-dependents) to find all dependents in an
index.)
@item(User guides:
@begin(list)
@item(How to use repositories as generic installation trackers)
@item(How to track dependencies between kubernetes services)
@item(How to track dependencies between cross-language builds)
@item(How to use dsolv to manage a project installation for development
purposes)
@end(list))
@end(list)

@end(section)

@end(section)
